Phase 1: Master the "Asynchronous" Flow
The Goal: Understand the Event Loop.
What to study: Promises, .then() vs async/await, and the fetch API.
Practice: Try fetching data from a public API (like the JSONPlaceholder or OpenWeather) instead of just your local folder.

Phase 2: Functional Logic & Array Methods
The Goal: Stop using for loops for everything.
What to study: .map(), .filter(), and .reduce().
Practice: Take your songs array and use .filter() to create a "Search" bar that only shows songs matching a specific letter.


Phase 3: DOM Manipulation & Events
You’re already doing this by injecting <li> tags. The next step is making it "cleaner."
The Goal: Manage the UI efficiently.
What to study: Event Delegation (handling clicks on 100 songs using only one listener), and "State Management" (tracking what is playing without relying on the HTML text).
Practice: Instead of using innerHTML +=, try using document.createElement and appendChild. It’s faster and more secure.


Phase 4: Understanding "Scope" and "This"
This is where most beginners get stuck. JavaScript behaves differently depending on where a variable is declared.
The Goal: Predict exactly what a variable equals at any time.
What to study: Closures, Arrow Functions vs Regular Functions, and Global vs Local scope.
Practice: Try moving your currentSong variable inside a function and see why the "Play" button suddenly stops working.